<ProductLayout title={page.title} hreflang={hreflang} description={page.description} canonical={page.canonical} ogImage="/images/flaxseeds-linseed-hulls-2.jpg" gallery={gallery} category={category} price={price} related={related} descriptionHtml={`<p>Flaxseed Hull (Linseed Hull) are derived from high-quality flaxseeds (<em>Linum usitatissimum</em>), containing a rich source of dietary fiber and lignans.</p><p>These hulls are widely used in health foods, animal feed, and functional food applications due to their exceptional nutritional properties.</p><p>Flaxseed Materials are from Inner Mongolia, China.</p><h2><strong>Production Process</strong></h2><ol><li><strong>Raw Material Selection</strong> – Sourced from premium, non-GMO flaxseeds to ensure purity and quality.</li><li><strong>Mechanical Separation</strong> – Physically separated from flaxseeds to retain maximum nutrients.</li><li><strong>Low-Temperature Drying</strong> – Preserves active compounds and extends shelf life.</li><li><strong>Milling &amp; Sieving</strong> – Available in various particle sizes (powder or flakes) according to customer requirements.</li></ol><h2><strong>Nutritional Composition</strong> (per 100g)</h2><ul><li><strong>Dietary Fiber</strong>: 45-55%</li><li><strong>Lignans</strong>: Up to 3-4%</li><li><strong>Protein</strong>: 10-15%</li><li><strong>Fat</strong>: 2-4%</li><li><strong>Moisture</strong>: ≤10%</li></ul><h2><strong>Key Applications</strong></h2><ol><li><strong>Food Industry</strong><ul><li><strong>Functional Foods</strong>: Used in granola bars, cereals, and protein powders for added fiber.</li><li><strong>Baking Products</strong>: Enhances texture and nutritional value in bread, cakes, and pastries.</li><li><strong>Beverage Supplements</strong>: Added to plant-based shakes and protein drinks.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Animal Feed</strong><ul><li><strong>Ruminant Feed (Cattle, Sheep)</strong>: Provides fermentable fiber to improve digestion.</li><li><strong>Pet Food</strong>: Incorporated into dog and cat food for digestive health benefits.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Functional Ingredient Extraction</strong><ul><li><strong>Lignan Extraction</strong>: Used in antioxidant supplements for its anti-inflammatory and estrogen-regulating properties.</li><li><strong>Dietary Fiber Supplements</strong>: Utilized as a fiber booster in various food formulations.</li></ul></li></ol><h2><strong>Specifications</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Appearance</strong>: Light brown to yellowish-brown powder/flakes</li><li><strong>Particle Size</strong>: Customizable (20-100 mesh)</li><li><strong>Moisture Content</strong>: ≤10%</li><li><strong>Lignan Content</strong>: 1-4% (customizable)</li><li><strong>Packaging</strong>: 25kg bags or bulk packaging, with customizable options available</li></ul><h2><strong>Storage &amp; Shelf Life</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>: Keep in a cool, dry, and well-ventilated place, away from direct sunlight.</li><li><strong>Shelf Life</strong>: 24 months (unopened, under proper storage conditions).</li></ul><h2><strong>Key Advantages</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>100% Natural</strong> – Free from artificial additives and preservatives</li><li><strong>High in Lignans &amp; Fiber</strong> – Supports the development of functional and health food products</li><li><strong>Customizable Particle Sizes</strong> – Suitable for various applications</li></ul><h2>FAQs about Flaxseed hull</h2><h3>1. <strong>What are flaxseed hulls?</strong></h3><p>Flaxseed hulls are the outer fiber-rich layer of flaxseeds, obtained as a byproduct after dehulling. They are rich in dietary fiber and lignans and are commonly used in food, animal feed, and dietary supplements.</p><h3>2. <strong>What are the health benefits of flaxseed hulls?</strong></h3><p>Flaxseed hulls are high in <strong>lignans</strong>, which have antioxidant and estrogen-regulating properties that support cardiovascular health, reduce inflammation, and promote gut health. Additionally, their high dietary fiber content aids digestion and enhances satiety.</p><h3>3. <strong>How are flaxseed hulls used in food and animal feed?</strong></h3><ul><li>In food, flaxseed hulls can be added to baked goods (such as bread and cookies) to increase dietary fiber content or used as a health food ingredient.</li><li>In animal feed, they serve as a high-quality fiber source that supports gut health, especially for ruminants, horses, and pet food.</li></ul><h3>4. <strong>Are flaxseed hulls gluten-free?</strong></h3><p>Yes. Flaxseed hulls are naturally <strong>gluten-free</strong>, making them suitable for individuals with gluten sensitivities and those following a low-carb diet.</p><h3>5. <strong>What is the difference between flaxseed hulls and flaxseed meal?</strong></h3><p>Flaxseed hulls are the fibrous outer layer of flaxseeds, rich in lignans and insoluble dietary fiber, whereas <strong>flaxseed meal</strong> is made from ground whole flaxseeds and contains more protein and oil.</p><h3>6. <strong>How should flaxseed hulls be stored to maintain freshness?</strong></h3><p>It is recommended to store flaxseed hulls in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>, away from direct sunlight. For long-term storage, keep them sealed and refrigerated to prevent oxidation and moisture damage.</p>`} />
